{"name":"Globe Thistle Grey Rose","hex":"#998d8d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"globe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any spherical (or nearly spherical) object.\n  The eyeball.","The eyeball."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Globe (“the earth”)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/globe"},{"term":"thistle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several perennial composite plants, especially of genera Cirsium, Carduus, Cynara, or Onopordum, having prickly leaves and showy flower heads with prickly bracts.","This plant seen as the national emblem of Scotland."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thistle"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Echinops","blocks":[{"text":"Echinops is a genus of about 130 species of flowering plants in the family Asteraceae, commonly known as globe thistles. They have spiny foliage and produce blue or white spherical flower heads. They are distributed from central Asia, Mongolia and north-eastern China to the Mediterranean basin, temperate regions of Eurasia, reaching to Indian subcontinent and tropical Africa. Globe thistles are a common host plant for weevils of the genus Larinus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Fuzzy-horned_bumblebee_%28Bombus_mixtus%29_on_globe_thistle_%28Echinops_sp.%29_Seward_Park.jpg/330px-Fuzzy-horned_bumblebee_%28Bombus_mixtus%29_on_globe_thistle_%28Echinops_sp.%29_Seward_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Echinops","wikidata":"Q650106"},{"title":"Globe","blocks":[{"text":"A globe is a spherical model of Earth, of some other celestial body, or of the celestial sphere. Globes serve purposes similar to maps, but, unlike maps, they do not distort the surface that they portray except to scale it down. A model globe of Earth is called a terrestrial globe. A model globe of the celestial sphere is called a celestial globe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/The_Great_Globe%2C_Guyot_Hall%2C_Princeton_University.jpg/330px-The_Great_Globe%2C_Guyot_Hall%2C_Princeton_University.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Globe","wikidata":"Q133792"},{"title":"Thistle","blocks":[{"text":"Thistle is the popular name of a group of flowering plants characterised by leaves with sharp spikes on the margins, mostly in the family Asteraceae. Prickles can also occur all over the plant – on the stem, and on the flat parts of the leaves. These prickles protect the plant from herbivores. Typically, an involucre with a clasping shape, similar to a cup or urn, subtends each of a thistle's flower heads. The typically feathery pappus of a ripe thistle flower is known as thistle-down."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Carduus_Nutans_in_the_Carpathian_Mountains.jpg/330px-Carduus_Nutans_in_the_Carpathian_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thistle","wikidata":"Q538906"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Illustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Illustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Illustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of globe thistle Echinops sphaerocephalus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Echinops_sphaerocephalus0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Echinops_ritro_01.jpg/500px-Echinops_ritro_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Echinops_ritro_01.jpg/1280px-Echinops_ritro_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Echinops_ritro_01.jpg/500px-Echinops_ritro_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Echinops ritro ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEchinops_ritro_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Echinops_adenocaulos_01.jpg/120px-Echinops_adenocaulos_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thu